<!--
    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 BorderKeys contributors

    A row of three keys over a space bar, drawn the way the keyboard draws its own: rounded
    rectangles, the pale label colour on the caps, the accent on the bar.

    Three caps rather than two, and one row rather than two, because both ends of that trade
    were rendered and looked at. Two wide caps read as a pair of dashes; two rows of three
    are the most obviously a keyboard at full size and merge into texture by the time a
    folder or the recents strip shrinks the icon to about 56 pixels. Three caps survive both.

    A vector rather than PNGs at five densities. minSdk is 30, so the adaptive icon is always
    available and no legacy raster fallback is needed; that leaves the icon as text the
    repository can diff, review and license, with no binary asset and no .license sidecar.

    Sizing is the part that is easy to get wrong. The 108dp viewport is the adaptive-icon
    canvas, of which a launcher shows about the middle 72dp and guarantees only the middle
    66dp. This mark occupies x 31..77 and y 36..72, so its farthest corner sits 29 of the
    permitted 36 units from the centre. A first attempt drew it at 60x60, inside the safe
    zone by the letter of the specification, and still wrong: scaled up to fill the mask it
    ran to the edges and read as an abstract flag rather than as a keyboard.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
